[Music]  china has been on a global vaccine  delivery drive since the early days of  the pandemic  and beijing gave that effort a boost  amid growing concerns over omicron a new  coronavirus variant that seems far more  transmissible than others  the pledge came after china said it  shipped 1.8 billion vaccines to 120  countries i'm very grateful of this is  this huge donation to africa china was  very interested in using its vaccines to  advance a number of geopolitical goals  and the u.s has its own goals too i  think the u.s based on its trials is  very competent in its vaccine they feel  like it's important for them to be in  lead donating  less than a week after chinese president  xi jinping's pledge president biden also  promised to send 9 million extra  vaccines to africa i'm keeping the  promise that america will become the  arsenal of vaccines china got out ahead  of the u.s in shipping its doses around  the world but the biden administration  has said it's donated more and now  omicron could challenge both superpowers  vaccine diplomacy efforts  but first how is china able to get a  head start  as early as may of 2020 beijing declared  that it would make its homegrown  vaccines a global public good and it was  able to make that promise because it  started vaccinating its own population  with its doses a few months later  then by september china started shipping  its vaccines abroad the us on the other  hand began sending doses nearly a year  later  china was faster because its vaccines  were made with traditional methods and  not with advanced genetic technology  like the first vaccines that were  approved in the u.s  this also meant chinese doses were  easier to ship since they didn't need to  be stored in ultra-cold temperatures  plus state enterprises jumped in to help  with the vaccine supply chain working  with 19 countries to manufacture doses  locally being the first is very  important because there's an element of  stickiness so in some ways makes it more  difficult for the recipient countries to  buy vaccines from other countries  afterwards bonnie lynn is a senior  fellow at the center for strategic and  international studies a bipartisan  foreign policy think tank she's been  tracking china's vaccine diplomacy and  found that of the 1.1 billion doses  beijing agreed to ship 96 were  designated as sales and the rest as  donations  china said its vaccines would be offered  at a fair and reasonable price and lynn  says this direct-to-consumer  relationship gives beijing leverage one  of the things that china does gain is a  bit more influence  over the recipient countries and china  was able to use its vaccines to  encourage countries to  issue statements in support of china for  instance paraguay said it was approached  by unofficial chinese brokers who  offered to purchase vaccines from china  on its behalf in return for breaking  diplomatic ties with taiwan it declined  the offer and china said the incident  was malicious disinformation  countries in the indo-pacific region  have also been receiving chinese doses  and that's grabbed the attention of  another vaccine donor the united states  has focused quite a bit of our efforts  in trying to provide vaccines in that  region in order to push back against  whatever influence china may have  and the u.s has been trying to lead by  donating its vaccines to poorer  countries and a majority of them are  from pfizer and biontech and moderna  which have high efficacy rates against  the original variant of the virus  compare that with china's vaccines  a study by bahrain and colombia  university also found that the death  rate for sino farm recipients was three  times higher than those inoculated with  pfizer  lin says the efficacy of chinese  vaccines may have dented beijing's  efforts to gain influence  a number of countries are  much more cautious now about taking  chinese vaccines and there are  increasing questions of why does china  need to have such a stringent zero  tolerance and many suspect it's because  of its lack of confidence in its own  vaccines  and now the emergence of a new  fast-spreading variant has triggered a  wave of fresh restrictions and omicron  is forcing leaders to speed up  vaccination drives around the world  individuals who talk within the  administration are really concerned like  unless we get everyone vaccinated new  variants will crop up i also think  there's been a fair amount of pressure  on the administration to do more  um to help with the donation and to help  lead a lot of the world efforts in about  a week of omicron being declared a  variant of concern by the who but the  u.s and china made pledges to ship more  vaccines  it's about what can we be done  collaboratively to try and bring the  pandemic to heal and i think just from  what we've seen so far especially with  this new variant that just becomes more  urgent but those efforts also rely on  how current vaccines respond to omicron  and when boosters could be available to  poorer countries a hong kong university  study found two doses of the sinovac  vaccine do not produce enough antibodies  to fight against a new variant  meanwhile pfizer and biontech said that  initial lab tests showed that two doses  were significantly less efficacious at  blocking infection but a third dose  neutralized omicron and moderna said a  third dose of its vaccine increased  immune responses against the new variant  so while both superpowers compete to  lead with vaccines it may ultimately  come down to how effective the doses are  against new emerging variants  [Music]  you

